WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
A connection exists and supports the machine’s required two kilowatts.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.200
The task needs eight hours, but supply is available for only four.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.550
Capacity and duration are separate conditions: a nominal connection does not prove effective access.

4
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:28.900
A line makes ten units per hour for eight hours: eighty planned units.

5
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:35.750
A two-hour outage removes twenty; restarting takes another hour without production.

6
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:43.100
Output falls to fifty: outage cost also includes recovery.

7
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:50.450
A fictional process must stay below six degrees and starts at four.

8
00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:57.300
During the outage, temperature rises to eight and crosses that limit.

9
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:04.650
Restoring power afterwards does not erase the interval outside specification.

10
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:12.000
Three tasks are pending, each requiring two kilowatt-hours.

11
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.850
Four kilowatt-hours complete two; six complete all three.

12
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:26.517
Going from six to eight adds no fourth task: demand is fixed here.

13
00:01:27.017 --> 00:01:35.317
Two loads of two and three kilowatts run together for one hour: combined peak power is five.

14
00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:42.167
Running them in separate hours reduces the peak to three kilowatts.

15
00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:49.517
Energy remains five kilowatt-hours; completion now takes two hours rather than one.

16
00:01:50.017 --> 00:01:56.867
Eight stored kilowatt-hours at ninety percent efficiency leave 7.2 usable.

17
00:01:56.867 --> 00:02:04.033
At two kilowatts, that energy would cover 3.6 hours if output power allowed it.

18
00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:11.383
An inverter limited to 1.5 kilowatts cannot supply that load even with enough energy.

